  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "Which of the following Diophantine equations have solutions?\n\nI. $14x-21y=8$\n\nII. $4x-16y=6$\n\nIII. $-2x+7y=5$",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that a linear Diophantine equation\n\n$$\nax+by=c\n$$\n\nhas a solution if and only if $d\\,|\\,c,$ where $d=\\text{gcd}(a,b).$  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each equation in turn.\n\n- Equation I has no solution. We have $a=14,$ $b=-21,$ and $c=8.$ Since\n\n$$\nd=\\text{gcd}(14,-21)=7\n$$\n\nand $7\\,\\text{⧸}|\\,8,$ our equation has no solution.\n\n- Equation II has no solution. We have $a=4,$ $b=-16,$ and $c=6.$ Since\n\n$$\nd=\\text{gcd}(4,-16)=4\n$$\n\nand $4\\,\\text{⧸}|\\,6,$ our equation has no solution.\n\n- Equation III has solutions. We have $a=-2,$ $b=7,$ and $c=5.$ Since\n\n$$\nd=\\text{gcd}(-2,7)=1\n$$\n\nand $1\\,|\\,5,$ our equation has solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
